<div class="mw-heading mw-heading2"><h2 id="Aftermath_and_legacy">Aftermath and legacy</h2><span class="mw-editsection"><span class="mw-editsection-bracket">[</span><a href="/w/index.php?title=German_invasion_of_Denmark_(1940)&amp;action=edit&amp;section=25" title="Edit section: Aftermath and legacy"><span>edit</span></a><span class="mw-editsection-bracket">]</span></span></div>
<style data-mw-deduplicate="TemplateStyles:r1236090951">.mw-parser-output .hatnote{font-style:italic}.mw-parser-output div.hatnote{padding-left:1.6em;margin-bottom:0.5em}.mw-parser-output .hatnote i{font-style:normal}.mw-parser-output .hatnote+link+.hatnote{margin-top:-0.5em}@media print{body.ns-0 .mw-parser-output .hatnote{display:none!important}}</style><div role="note" class="hatnote navigation-not-searchable">Main article: <a href="/wiki/Occupation_of_Denmark" class="mw-redirect" title="Occupation of Denmark">Occupation of Denmark</a></div>
<p>The Danes capitulated within six hours, resulting in a uniquely lenient <a href="/wiki/Occupation_of_Denmark" class="mw-redirect" title="Occupation of Denmark">occupation</a>, as the Germans were content to leave the <a href="/wiki/Nordic_race" title="Nordic race">Aryan</a> Danes to manage their own affairs. Danish soldiers were disarmed that afternoon, and those captured were allowed to return to their units.<sup id="cite_ref-FOOTNOTEFinsted2004_14-18" class="reference"><a href="#cite_note-FOOTNOTEFinsted2004-14"><span class="cite-bracket">&#91;</span>14<span class="cite-bracket">&#93;</span></a></sup> The following day, the island of <a href="/wiki/Bornholm" title="Bornholm">Bornholm</a> was occupied without incident.<sup id="cite_ref-FOOTNOTEZiemke195960_43-0" class="reference"><a href="#cite_note-FOOTNOTEZiemke195960-43"><span class="cite-bracket">&#91;</span>43<span class="cite-bracket">&#93;</span></a></sup> The Royal Danish Army was severely reduced after the invasion, with only a 3,300-strong "Life Guard" unit allowed to remain.<sup id="cite_ref-FOOTNOTENigel20146_44-0" class="reference"><a href="#cite_note-FOOTNOTENigel20146-44"><span class="cite-bracket">&#91;</span>44<span class="cite-bracket">&#93;</span></a></sup> Many Danish merchant ships were caught out abroad following the sudden and rapid invasion. Approximately 240 of these ships were incorporated into the Allied <a href="/wiki/Merchant_navy" title="Merchant navy">merchant navy</a> while ships still in Danish ports served the Germans in transporting iron ore.<sup id="cite_ref-FOOTNOTEHolbraad201783-84_45-0" class="reference"><a href="#cite_note-FOOTNOTEHolbraad201783-84-45"><span class="cite-bracket">&#91;</span>45<span class="cite-bracket">&#93;</span></a></sup> A handful of Danish soldiers and pilots escaped to the United Kingdom either directly by plane or via neutral Sweden. Those who escaped either served amongst the <a href="/wiki/Royal_Air_Force" title="Royal Air Force">RAF</a> or <a href="/wiki/Special_Operations_Executive" title="Special Operations Executive">SOE</a>.<sup id="cite_ref-FOOTNOTEHolbraad2017125_46-0" class="reference"><a href="#cite_note-FOOTNOTEHolbraad2017125-46"><span class="cite-bracket">&#91;</span>46<span class="cite-bracket">&#93;</span></a></sup>
</p><p>Following the reported peaceful occupation of Denmark, Allied views on Denmark were contemptuous. It was alleged that a boxing commentator had said "without fighting—like a Dane" concerning a first-round knockout.<sup id="cite_ref-FOOTNOTEOsborn_47-0" class="reference"><a href="#cite_note-FOOTNOTEOsborn-47"><span class="cite-bracket">&#91;</span>47<span class="cite-bracket">&#93;</span></a></sup> Danes have since adopted the saying "<span title="Danish-language text"><i lang="da">Aldrig mere 9 April</i></span>" (<abbr title="translation">transl.</abbr><span>&#8201;Never again an April 9</span>).<sup id="cite_ref-FOOTNOTEOsborn_47-1" class="reference"><a href="#cite_note-FOOTNOTEOsborn-47"><span class="cite-bracket">&#91;</span>47<span class="cite-bracket">&#93;</span></a></sup>
</p>
